  <front>
    <title>IPv6 Prefix Length Recommendation for Forwarding</title>
    <seriesInfo name="RFC" value="7608" stream="IETF"/>
    <seriesInfo name="BCP" value="198"/>
    <author fullname="M. Boucadair" initials="M." surname="Boucadair">
      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">France Telecom</organization>
      <address/>
    </author>
    <author fullname="A. Petrescu" initials="A." surname="Petrescu">
      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">CEA, LIST</organization>
      <address/>
    </author>
    <author fullname="F. Baker" initials="F." surname="Baker">
      <organization showOnFrontPage="true">Cisco Systems</organization>
      <address/>
    </author>
    <date year="2015" month="July"/>
    <abstract pn="section-abstract">
      <t indent="0" pn="section-abstract-1">IPv6 prefix length, as in IPv4, is a parameter conveyed and used in
IPv6 routing and forwarding processes in accordance with the
Classless Inter-domain Routing (CIDR) architecture.  The length of an
IPv6 prefix may be any number from zero to 128, although subnets
using stateless address autoconfiguration (SLAAC) for address
allocation conventionally use a /64 prefix.  Hardware and software
implementations of routing and forwarding should therefore impose no
rules on prefix length, but implement longest-match-first on prefixes
of any valid length.</t>
    </abstract>
